 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ for building the KD-tree and $O(N)$ for querying neighbors, where $N$ is the number of SfM points.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the SfM points and the resulting triangles.
-- **Strengths:** Provides geometrically meaningful initial primitives that align with the scene's local structure, potentially leading to faster convergence and better reconstruction quality compared to purely random initialization.
-- **Limitations:** Heavily dependent on the quality and density of the SfM point cloud. Poor SfM results (e.g., missing points, outliers) will lead to poor initialization.
+- **Time Complexity:** Initialization is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $K$ is the number of points used for nearest neighbor search (typically small, e.g., 3-10). If FPS is used, it adds $O(N^2)$ or $O(N \log N)$ depending on implementation.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing vertices and attributes.
+- **Strengths:** Geometrically aware initialization; adapts to local scene density; avoids the "blobby" artifacts of large-variance random init.
+- **Limitations:** Requires high-quality SfM point cloud; sensitive to SfM noise/outliers; [S2, unofficial] suggests it may require more iterations or specific loss terms for texture.
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in "Triangle Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" [S1]. It was introduced as an alternative to random initialization for triangle-based splatting, leveraging the geometric information available from SfM reconstructions.
+Originated in the context of differentiable triangle splatting. [S1] (Triangle Splatting) proposes this initialization to replace Gaussian initialization with triangle primitives. [S2, unofficial] (DiffSoup) adapts it for extreme simplification, using a hybrid sampling strategy.
 
